narrative=[
 'Axios reports that Bukele called Trump on Tuesday before the surge and was told, "Do what you have to do," with a warning that "it might get messy." Up to 2,000 Salvadoran troops are going in under a bilateral agreement Haiti approved on Wednesday, and Axios\'s sources say the number "could double or triple"; local counts already run to about 2,500. The same report says Washington turned down Bukele\'s requests for drones and transport planes and that Rubio wants Haiti "off the radar," while Jake Johnston of CEPR says US and Colombian military planes helped with logistics and personnel flights are hidden from public trackers.',
 'The Dominican Republic is the back door. Listín Diario watched seven helicopters, five Salvadoran MD500Es and two Black Hawks, refuel and take on weapons at Jimaní on 29 Oct before flying into Haiti. Bukele says his troops will go home the day the four missing technicians are returned alive; if not, "we will rescue them the hard way, and along the way we will finish them off."',
 'The effort to crush the gangs scored its clearest result outside the capital. On 29 Oct the police, backed by the Task Force, killed the Cabaret gang leader known as "Galaxie" and about ten of his men at Bercy, between Cabaret and Arcahaie (Le Nouvelliste; the Arcahaie mayor, via Magazine Haïti, says kamikaze drones were used). Accounts differ on who took part: HaitiInfoProj names the Arcahaie brigade, and Rolfy Martínez names the Gang Suppression Force and El Salvador. Axios says Erik Prince\'s Vectus works alongside the Salvadorans; Vectus itself has said nothing.',
 "The gangs are digging in. 400 Mawozo and Chen Mechan blocked the roads of Rémy with rocks, Radio Télé Confiance reports on Chen Mechan forcing children to carry guns, and Lanmò San Jou's new videos mock Bukele's prisons while talking of peace. A diaspora group, UNA-Haiti, accuses the Salvadorans of bombing civilians in Croix-des-Bouquets and Canaan; no outlet on the ground reports such strikes so far.",
 "Haiti's own forces grew by 1,189 police graduates on 30 Oct, and the US Embassy says its program has now trained more than 3,200. Canada joined the EU in saying sanctioned people who fund gangs have no place in the elections.",
]
